# Erdős Problem #99 — low-budget weighted overlap raises the atomic train gap to 0.00154

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only strengthening of WO145  
**External input:** none beyond the existing stopped-train, finite Mantel, TL69 pair-payment, and Section-163 ordered-path stack

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

WO145 proves `eta_grid=0.00145` by charging the majority-deletion cost against the
same-label missing budget.  In the actual low-budget regime produced by the dominant
orbit, that charge is stronger than the sharp global WO145 inequality.

If `u` is the leaf-label mass deleted from the center shore and `v` is the center-label
mass deleted from the leaf shore, put

\[
q:=u+{v\over2}.
\]

WO145 gives `q^2 <= 9p/4`, where `p` is the same-label missing budget.  Since the full
Mantel missing budget is already very small, this forces `q<=s/3`, where `s` is the
trimmed mass of each Mantel shore.  In that range the majority signs imply the much
stronger inequality

\[
\boxed{p\ge q^2.}
\]

Feeding this into the same two-leaf payment argument yields

\[
\boxed{
 x\sigma\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00154,
 \qquad
 \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{LB154.1}
\]

Hence, at the same proof-only scope,

\[
\boxed{\eta_{grid}=0.00154.}
\tag{LB154.2}
\]

The finite inequality below has its first numerical zero near
`0.00154671967318`, so the stated constant stays below the method ceiling with an
exact interval margin.

---

## 1. Dominant-orbit setup

Put

\[
s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\eta:=0.00154,
\qquad
\bar A:=2s_2+\eta.
\tag{LB154.3}
\]

Assume for contradiction that the total absolute atomic train mass satisfies

\[
A:=x\sigma\le\bar A.
\tag{LB154.4}
\]

Use the same dominant three-direction orbit and maximum-degree Mantel partition as
audited Section 168, AG47, AG58, TL69, LA90, and WO145.  Define

\[
M_0={4s_2^2\over\bar A},
\qquad
R_0=\bar A-M_0,
\tag{LB154.5}
\]

and

\[
d_0={\bar A^2\over4}-s_2^2+{R_0^2\over4}.
\tag{LB154.6}
\]

Each Mantel shore contains dominant-orbit atomic mass at least

\[
\boxed{
s_0={M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0}.
}
\tag{LB154.7}
\]

Trim the three atom populations on each shore until both shore totals are exactly
`s_0`.  Write the resulting masses as

\[
x_1+x_2+x_3=s_0,
\qquad
y_1+y_2+y_3=s_0.
\tag{LB154.8}
\]

Let `M` be the normalized total missing cross-shore pair mass in this trimmed system.
The Mantel partition gives

\[
\boxed{M\le2d_0.}
\tag{LB154.9}
\]

Same-window pairs cannot form strict-rhombus crossings, so

\[
\boxed{
p:=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+x_3y_3\le M.
}
\tag{LB154.10}
\]

---

## 2. Majority assignment and the low-budget variable

Majority-assign each label to a shore on which it has weakly larger mass.  As in
WO145, the exact certificate gives `s_0^2/3>2d_0`, so neither retained shore can be
empty.

After relabeling labels and possibly swapping shores, `X` contains one center label
and `Y` contains one or two leaf labels:

\[
x_1\ge y_1,
\qquad y_2\ge x_2,
\qquad y_3\ge x_3.
\tag{LB154.11}
\]

Set

\[
u:=x_2+x_3,
\qquad v:=y_1,
\qquad q:=u+{v\over2}.
\tag{LB154.12}
\]

Then

\[
x_1=s_0-u,
\qquad y_2+y_3=s_0-v.
\tag{LB154.13}
\]

WO145 proved the global weighted-deletion inequality

\[
q^2\le{9\over4}p.
\tag{LB154.14}
\]

Together with `p<=M<=2d_0`, this gives

\[
q\le {3\over2}\sqrt{2d_0}.
\tag{LB154.15}
\]

The exact interval certificate verifies

\[
{3\over2}\sqrt{2d_0}
<s_0/3.
\tag{LB154.16}
\]

Therefore every hypothetical survivor under (LB154.4) lies in the low-budget regime

\[
\boxed{q<s_0/3.}
\tag{LB154.17}
\]

---

## 3. Low-budget lemma: the same-label product pays `q^2`

### Lemma LB154.1

Under (LB154.11)--(LB154.13), if

\[
q=u+v/2\le s_0/3,
\]

then

\[
\boxed{p\ge q^2.}
\tag{LB154.18}
\]

### Proof

Write

\[
e_2:=y_2-x_2\ge0,
\qquad e_3:=y_3-x_3\ge0.
\]

Their sum is

\[
w:=e_2+e_3=s_0-u-v.
\tag{LB154.19}
\]

The low-budget assumption gives

\[
3u+{3v\over2}\le s_0,
\]

so

\[
w=s_0-u-v\ge2u+{v\over2}\ge2u.
\tag{LB154.20}
\]

Relabel leaves if necessary so `x_2<=x_3`.  Then, for fixed `x_2,x_3,w`, the leaf
product is minimized by placing all excess `w` on the smaller coefficient `x_2`:

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_2y_2+x_3y_3
&=x_2^2+x_3^2+x_2e_2+x_3e_3\\
&\ge x_2^2+x_3^2+x_2w.
\end{aligned}
\tag{LB154.21}
\]

Since `x_2+x_3=u` and `w>=2u>=2x_3`,

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_2^2+x_3^2+x_2w-u^2
&=x_2(w-2x_3)\ge0.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{x_2y_2+x_3y_3\ge u^2.}
\tag{LB154.22}
\]

The center product is

\[
x_1y_1=(s_0-u)v.
\]

Therefore

\[
p\ge u^2+(s_0-u)v.
\tag{LB154.23}
\]

Subtract

\[
q^2=\left(u+{v\over2}\right)^2
=u^2+uv+{v^2\over4}.
\]

The difference is

\[
v\left(s_0-2u-{v\over4}\right).
\]

Again `3u+3v/2<=s_0`, so

\[
s_0-2u-{v\over4}
\ge u+{5v\over4}\ge0.
\]

Thus `p>=q^2`.  `square`

The point is that a sufficiently small weighted majority deletion cannot be hidden in
one cheap same-label pair: the large unused leaf mass forces the leaf products to pay
quadratically.

---

## 4. Reinsert the two-leaf missing budget

Put

\[
K:={2\over\sqrt3},
\qquad L:=4+\sqrt2,
\qquad B:={3s_0\over2}-K.
\tag{LB154.24}
\]

After majority deletion the center mass is `a=s_0-u`, while the leaf masses `b,c`
satisfy `b+c=s_0-v`.  TL69 applies separately to the two center-to-leaf pairs and
convexity gives

\[
\boxed{
\rho_b+\rho_c
\ge {2\over L^2}(B-q)_+^2.
}
\tag{LB154.25}
\]

The same-label pairs counted by `p` and the two distinct-label pair sets counted by
`rho_b,rho_c` are disjoint.  Thus

\[
M\ge p+\rho_b+\rho_c.
\tag{LB154.26}
\]

Apply Lemma LB154.1:

\[
\boxed{
M\ge q^2+{2\over L^2}(B-q)_+^2.
}
\tag{LB154.27}
\]

The right side is minimized over all `q>=0` at

\[
q_*={2B\over L^2+2}<B,
\]

with minimum

\[
\boxed{
M\ge {2B^2\over L^2+2}.
}
\tag{LB154.28}
\]

Consequently every feasible survivor would have to satisfy

\[
\boxed{
2d_0\ge
{2(3s_0/2-2/\sqrt3)^2
 \over(4+\sqrt2)^2+2}.
}
\tag{LB154.29}
\]

---

## 5. Exact contradiction at `eta=0.00154`

The exact interval certificate gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\bar A&=1.906165613727914\ldots,\\
M_0&=1.903086857901024\ldots,\\
R_0&=0.003078755826889\ldots,\\
d_0&=0.001469524306930\ldots,\\
s_0&=0.913209053946726\ldots,\\
B&=0.215113042540838\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{LB154.30}
\]

It also certifies

\[
{3\over2}\sqrt{2d_0}
=0.0813194895531755\ldots
< s_0/3
=0.304403017982242\ldots,
\tag{LB154.31}
\]

so the low-budget lemma applies uniformly.

The forced missing budget is

\[
{2B^2\over(4+\sqrt2)^2+2}
>0.00295548648111587,
\tag{LB154.32}
\]

whereas the entire available Mantel budget is

\[
2d_0<0.00293904861386180.
\tag{LB154.33}
\]

The outward-certified gap exceeds

\[
1.6\times10^{-5}.
\tag{LB154.34}
\]

Contradiction.  Therefore (LB154.1) holds.

A high-precision solution of equality in (LB154.29) occurs near

\[
\eta_*=0.00154671967318022\ldots .
\tag{LB154.35}
\]

Thus `0.00154` is close to the ceiling of this low-budget refinement.

---

## 6. Immediate consequences and scope

Every proof-only propagation depending only on the atomic train gain may now use
`g=0.00154`.  In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00154,
}
\tag{LB154.36}
\]

and the rhombic-only transfer gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.00308.
}
\tag{LB154.37}
\]

The correlated identity similarly gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}\ge0.00308.
}
\tag{LB154.38}
\]

Scope is unchanged from WO145: canonical full-core Type-II train-measure limits, all
source points retained, no source deletion, no strict-diameter competitor, and no
claim of C-II or Level-I closure.

The exact interval certificate is
`research/verify_target_c_lowbudget_weighted_overlap_atomic_gap_00154.py`.

**Disposition:** proof-only LB154 candidate awaiting independent audit.  C-II remains open.
